WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:02.252
You'll have to raise a rib.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:03.461
[panting]

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:05.880
[panting]

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.966
Operating on yourself for little wounds,

00:00:07.966 --> 00:00:10.301
if you have anesthesia,
doable, with mirrors.

00:00:10.301 --> 00:00:12.053
I've done it a couple times on myself.

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.054
Hi, I'm Peter Rhee.

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:15.640
I'm a trauma surgeon for about 30 years,

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.184
and I also served about 24 years

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.602
with the United States Navy.

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:22.480
I'm back to look at more
battle-wound scenes in movies

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.441
and judge how real they are.

00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:29.279
OK.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:30.113
[gunshot]

00:00:30.113 --> 00:00:30.989
[gasping]

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:33.533
I give him a lot of points
for the way that he dropped,

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.619
and that's what almost
everybody does in real life,

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:38.246
is when you get shot, you just drop.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:41.041
It wasn't these dramatic Hollywood type of

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:42.959
blown-back-from-a-little-handgun
type of thing.

00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:45.336
I'm going to take away points
for how big the wounds are.

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:48.089
A sniper usually uses a bolt-action

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:49.215
hunting type of rifle

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.594
about three-tenths of an inch in diameter.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:54.679
If you shoot them, it'll make a tiny hole.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:57.682
I had one guy who was
sentry, Marine Corps.

00:00:57.682 --> 00:00:59.642
It was from the sniper of a Ramadi,

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:01.352
and we did a thoracotomy on him.

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.481
So he bled out his entire
cavity into the chest,

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:07.275
but the entry and exit, tiny little hole.

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:08.443
No blood on the skin.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:09.611
Covering fire!

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:12.530
What happened?

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.364
Sniper.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:14.574
They didn't waste time,

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.326
they just dragged him out of there.

00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:17.952
He's not moving his arms and legs,

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:19.913
so he could've hit the spinal cord,

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.332
but the trajectory looks like it did not.

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:23.541
'Cause right there, from that,

00:01:23.541 --> 00:01:26.377
the moulage, it's more
of the trapezius muscles

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:27.962
rather than the carotids.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:29.881
Overall, you know,

00:01:29.881 --> 00:01:32.258
what I was criticizing
was too small and minor.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.593
I'll give this a seven.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.428
He's way past go. Who's next?

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.846
Over here, sir!

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:38.973
Col. Moody, sir.

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:41.142
Yeah. Mm-hmm. Most of
our tents look like that.

00:01:41.142 --> 00:01:43.311
The Marine Corps started
doing this early in my career.

00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:45.188
We started outfitting those things

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.232
and deciding all the little instruments

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.442
and gauzes that we were
going to put into it.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.403
And then the Army has these
tents that, put together,

00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:53.947
you can make bigger and bigger spaces.

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:57.200
But they had very nice
fabricated metal shelves in there

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.661
and things like that. Those
things are not available.

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:00.995
GSW to the abdomen.

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:03.206
Let's stabilize until we get him to an OR.

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:05.834
Give me some wet bandages
and an occlusive dressing.

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:08.169
All out of those. I need a laryngoscope,

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:09.546
an ET tube. I need to intubate.

00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:12.924
So, it looks like it's a triage area.

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:15.135
But what I didn't like right away,

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:18.304
he starts barking orders
to that woman behind him.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:22.308
He's talking to her like
she's his assistant,

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.726
and she's got her own patient.

00:02:23.726 --> 00:02:26.604
And then what he has is
a GSW to the abdomen,

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:29.524
which is a gunshot wound to the torso,

00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:30.942
to the abdomen itself.

00:02:30.942 --> 00:02:32.193
And then the lady tells you

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:35.196
that she's got an emergent
airway that she has to do.

00:02:35.196 --> 00:02:38.158
Very rare from trauma unless
you've got facial injuries.

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.909
Moody, sir. Sandstorm's coming.

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:41.161
Command's ordered us to move back.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:43.746
Hunt. We'll take your GSW in the APC.

00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:47.792
So, not very realistic.

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:50.420
When I was in Iraq,
when we got sandstorms,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.089
there were pretty bizarre
stuff that that would come in.

00:02:53.089 --> 00:02:55.175
And what you do is you hang tight.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:57.260
Don't drive, stay indoors.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.012
Doesn't make sense to
me that they're going to

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.724
take everybody there in
that situation and scenario,

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:05.602
get into APCs, which is
armored personnel carriers,

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:07.896
and they just get into
ambulances is all it is.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.398
By the time they load them
into the vehicles, trust me,

00:03:10.398 --> 00:03:12.483
it's going to take well over
an hour, an hour and a half.

00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:13.651
So during that time period,

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:15.904
those patients would be dying as well.

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:17.697
[explosion]

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:21.284
[loud moaning]

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:23.786
So, this is a common scenario

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:25.496
where you would get an IED.

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:27.123
When you're inside these vehicles

00:03:27.123 --> 00:03:29.125
and you get blown up from underneath,

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:31.169
they actually don't get
penetrating injuries.

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:32.212
You get blunt trauma.

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:33.504
So when the guys come out,

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:36.382
the guy has a lot of
fragmentation injuries.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:37.425
Got it.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.344
The hand was swelling so much

00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:40.762
it would've popped the finger right off.

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:42.889
Save the arm. You gotta tourniquet it.

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:46.184
[screams]

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:47.268
None of this makes sense.

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:50.730
He's got an obvious neck
injury, which is high priority.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.774
So if they're dying to have somebody

00:03:52.774 --> 00:03:53.733
with blood in their mouth,

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.193
this would be a time to do it. Right?

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:57.195
There's three things we're
worried about in the neck.

00:03:57.195 --> 00:03:58.571
That's your carotid artery,

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.448
your trachea, and your esophagus.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.491
He's trying to tell him to

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:03.826
cut the arm off with a tourniquet,

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:05.703
and he was fussing around with a ring.

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:07.038
Again, life over limb.

00:04:07.038 --> 00:04:09.249
And then, he's got plenty of tourniquets.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.458
That's an ambulance.

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:13.169
And he uses his web belt from his pants.

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:14.963
I give this a two. Two out of 10.

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:21.886
I love this scene.

00:04:21.886 --> 00:04:24.597
This wound was created by a sharp stick.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.890
Eventually, the main thing is that

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.141
it's going to get infected.

00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:29.727
Wood is notorious for just having

00:04:29.727 --> 00:04:31.688
all sorts of germs and bacteria in it.

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:36.025
So getting it out reduces the
burden of the bacterial load

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:37.986
and the fungus load and everything else.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:46.286
Go Sylvester!

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:47.245
I was freaking out here

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:49.247
'cause it looked like he's
going to burn his biceps.

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:51.416
When we do electrocautery in the surgery,

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:53.751
it's pretty brutal,
and we do actually burn

00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:54.794
a lot of the tissue.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:56.004
So I wouldn't mind doing that

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:58.131
if the skin holes were bigger.

00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:00.008
But his skin holes are usually tiny,

00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:02.802
so not like he's got a big
gaping wound in the back.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:04.887
When you put gunpowder inside there,

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:07.765
I have no experience on
this whatsoever myself,

00:05:07.765 --> 00:05:09.517
but I'm just using my imagination,

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:10.852
that when you light it,

00:05:10.852 --> 00:05:13.229
it's going to go this way, not this way.

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.066
So you are going to cause more
tissue injury inside as well.

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:18.985
Using gunpowder to cauterize

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.529
and also to sterilize is theoretical,

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:23.656
but I think that's pretty hokey.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.116
I'd give this about a four.

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:26.617
Don't try this at home!

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:35.877
The kid's got an infected arm.

00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:38.796
But he seemed very bright in this scene,

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:42.050
so he was very aware,
which is unfortunate,

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.052
because he's going to get the arm cut off

00:05:44.052 --> 00:05:46.554
while he's awake, without
anesthesia of any type.

00:05:48.056 --> 00:05:48.890
It's all right.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:53.561
The little knife

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:55.438
makes it look like one
slice and it's done.

00:05:55.438 --> 00:05:58.191
It's not. First, you gotta
slice the fleshy tissue.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:01.319
In the forearm, it's got
muscle and stuff inside

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:02.987
protected by the bones.

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:05.907
Can't use a slicing type of
knife to go through the bone.

00:06:05.907 --> 00:06:07.367
You gotta have a saw.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.035
We use what's called a Gigli saw,

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.704
which is a little cutting wire,

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.081
or we actually use a saw.

00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:14.832
The best saw is a hacksaw,

00:06:14.832 --> 00:06:17.210
but the surgical saws are going to be OK.

00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:24.717
I've never caused that kind of pain,

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.344
where I gave them something to bite on.

00:06:26.344 --> 00:06:28.012
Normally what we do in that situation

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:30.640
is we open up the wounds
and let it puss out.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.934
And they don't need to
necessarily amputate the arm.

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:34.352
But life over limb.

00:06:34.352 --> 00:06:36.771
You will amputate the arm
in order to save a life.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:39.774
I'm assuming that he might've
had a fracture underneath it

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:42.527
or they tried to get it
to heal and they couldn't.

00:06:42.527 --> 00:06:43.903
You'll have to raise a rib.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:47.532
[panting]

00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:49.617
Operating on yourself for little wounds,

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:51.953
if you have anesthesia,
doable, with mirrors.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:53.704
I've done it a couple times on myself.

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:56.624
They even did that in the 1800s.

00:06:56.624 --> 00:06:59.127
The issue that I have
with this particular clip

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:00.962
was raising the rib.

00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:03.172
If you hit the rib,
it's very, very painful.

00:07:03.172 --> 00:07:04.841
The ribs are extremely sensitive.

00:07:05.842 --> 00:07:07.218
Hi, sir.

00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:08.886
She'll patch up nicely.

00:07:08.886 --> 00:07:11.389
And what he was really doing
is going after the bullet.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:14.600
We normally don't take out
all the bullets that we find.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.437
If it's easily palpable
and we can find it,

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:19.689
it's in soft tissue, then
we'll pluck them out.

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:22.733
But most of the bullets do OK in the body.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:25.319
But in the movies, they
always make it look like

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:27.405
if you get the bullet out, you'll live,

00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:29.282
and if you don't take the
bullet out, you'll die.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:31.701
I'd give this a good
seven or eight out of 10.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.247
More sulfur, Mellish.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.169
It's good. It's good overall.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:42.628
They're pouring sulfur on it.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:45.423
It is an antibiotic.
It's an antibacterial.

00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:48.301
If you got a fractured leg,
you got a gash or wound,

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:50.344
if you put sulfur on it, it helps.

00:07:50.344 --> 00:07:51.929
Doesn't really stop the bleeding.

00:07:51.929 --> 00:07:55.099
It wasn't meant to be a
hemostatic agent, as you call it.

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:57.351
Am I shot in the spine?

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:01.022
Am I shot in the spine?

00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:03.065
He keeps saying, "Am I shot in the spine?"

00:08:03.065 --> 00:08:04.525
That's so smart of him to say that.

00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.903
And if he is, he's paralyzed.

00:08:06.903 --> 00:08:08.654
Paralyzed legs won't kill him.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:09.864
But if he's bleeding a lot,

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:11.782
and the spinal injuries bleed a lot,

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:12.992
that's what's going to kill him.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:14.827
Easy, easy.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.037
Put some pressure on it.

00:08:16.037 --> 00:08:18.748
Wait, I got an exit wound.

00:08:18.748 --> 00:08:21.209
How big is the hole?

00:08:21.209 --> 00:08:22.376
About the size of an acorn.

00:08:22.376 --> 00:08:24.420
The medic, he knows that he's paralyzed,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.089
and that's why he says,
"Check for a hole in my back."

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:29.467
And if it's big enough to
be the acorn, like he says,

00:08:29.467 --> 00:08:31.177
that means that it was
probably an exit wound.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:32.929
It really makes a huge difference

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:35.473
whether it's a handgun or if it's a rifle.

00:08:35.473 --> 00:08:38.601
The guns that they used
to use in World War II

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:41.229
that Germany had, there were two kinds.

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:44.649
One was from a rifle and a machine gun,

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:48.569
which are these large
bullets which are very fast,

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:52.031
but they'll make tiny little
holes actually on the entry.

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.950
But in the exit, they'll blow it out.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.409
They carry submachine guns.

00:08:55.409 --> 00:08:56.577
The bullet coming out of there

00:08:56.577 --> 00:08:58.704
is no different than a handgun.

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:02.833
So those will typically not
create as much of an exit wound.

00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:05.169
If it's a rifle, when
it goes out the back,

00:09:05.169 --> 00:09:06.504
it's a large hole.

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:08.631
And it's also called a cavitation wound.

00:09:08.631 --> 00:09:12.093
Overall, I'd give
this an eight out of 10.

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:14.762
Battalion aid says he's
got an open chest wound.

00:09:14.762 --> 00:09:17.306
There's blood pouring
through the pressure bandage.

00:09:17.306 --> 00:09:20.101
I think he's got a lacerated aorta.

00:09:20.101 --> 00:09:23.062
He diagnosed that because
the dressings are bleeding

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:24.855
that he's got a hole in the aorta.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:26.566
It could be coming from anywhere.

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:28.568
I can't get in there. You got a knife?

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:31.529
As soon as I get my hand in there,

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:33.864
I'm going to compress the
aorta against his spinal column

00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:34.991
and try to stop the bleeding.

00:09:34.991 --> 00:09:36.659
If he came in with a hole

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:39.370
that you could fit your hand into,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:40.621
then you can go in there

00:09:40.621 --> 00:09:43.207
and manually compress the aorta blindly

00:09:43.207 --> 00:09:45.626
at whatever area that you want to.

00:09:45.626 --> 00:09:47.962
So, he's going to take a knife

00:09:47.962 --> 00:09:49.547
and make the skin incision bigger.

00:09:49.547 --> 00:09:52.216
But you can't get your
hand in between the ribs.

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:54.885
You have to actually split
the muscle all the way

00:09:54.885 --> 00:09:56.429
front to back on the ribs

00:09:56.429 --> 00:09:58.472
in order to get a retractor,

00:09:58.472 --> 00:10:00.474
which is a vise, reverse vise,

00:10:00.474 --> 00:10:01.726
that splits your ribs open

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:03.811
so we can get into the chest cavity

00:10:03.811 --> 00:10:05.855
so we can put a clamp on the aorta.

00:10:05.855 --> 00:10:07.481
We cut off the blood to his spinal cord

00:10:07.481 --> 00:10:08.399
for more than 20 minutes,

00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:10.943
he stands a good chance
of winding up paralyzed.

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:12.737
When you put a clamp on the aorta,

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:14.113
he's right, if you do it too long,

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:16.907
then your organs below the clamp will die.

00:10:16.907 --> 00:10:19.285
But it preserves the
blood from above the clamp

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:20.661
to keep your heart and brain alive.

00:10:21.704 --> 00:10:23.039
Step through.

00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:25.625
Get his head and shoulders
flat, right on the floor.

00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:27.335
Klinger, we're going to need more ice!

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:29.462
When they cool him from the bucket of ice,

00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:32.214
that's called external passive cooling,

00:10:32.214 --> 00:10:33.549
where you let the heat go

00:10:33.549 --> 00:10:35.926
from your body to the
ice and melt the ice.

00:10:35.926 --> 00:10:38.054
That takes a long time to cool the core.

00:10:38.054 --> 00:10:39.722
So it's a waste of time.

00:10:39.722 --> 00:10:42.516
You have to do what's called
active internal cooling.

00:10:42.516 --> 00:10:44.602
You can only do it with
a pump or something.

00:10:44.602 --> 00:10:46.270
It would go to every cell in your brain,

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.481
every cell in your heart,
every cell in your body.

00:10:48.481 --> 00:10:50.858
And that's a very efficient
way to exchange heat.

00:10:51.067 --> 00:10:52.735
Here's the graft, doctor.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:53.694
Here goes.

00:10:55.404 --> 00:10:59.075
If you stick a piece of aorta
from another person into him

00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:01.619
that will give you
maybe three or four days

00:11:01.619 --> 00:11:04.330
before that blows out,
because it's not your aorta.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:05.998
It's not been matched.

00:11:05.998 --> 00:11:07.583
And when it's the wrong transplant,

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:09.669
it will blow out, and you'll die.

00:11:09.669 --> 00:11:11.629
From a feasibility medical standpoint,

00:11:11.629 --> 00:11:13.923
this is, like, a 1 ½ to two.

00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:17.218
Appears to be lodged between
the femur and the muscle.

00:11:17.218 --> 00:11:18.386
We're on our way up right now.

00:11:18.386 --> 00:11:20.096
OK, so, pause here for a second.

00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:22.181
Who put those clamps on there, OK?

00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:23.432
And what are they clamping?

00:11:23.432 --> 00:11:25.601
And where did he get the X-ray from?

00:11:25.601 --> 00:11:27.812
How did he notice it was her X-ray?

00:11:27.812 --> 00:11:30.690
By the way, the X-ray of a little old lady

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:34.735
with hardly any soft
tissue and has no fracture.

00:11:34.735 --> 00:11:38.072
She's got a 20-millimeter
unexploded ordnance in her leg.

00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:38.906
What?

00:11:38.906 --> 00:11:41.450
There's a 4-meter blast radius.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:42.993
Any movement could set it off.

00:11:42.993 --> 00:11:47.707
He acts like this is going
to be a fusing of a bomb

00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:50.084
that if you hit the wrong
fuse, it's going to go up.

00:11:50.084 --> 00:11:52.044
This is a 20 millimeter.

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:56.257
20 millimeters are what
they use in Vulcan guns,

00:11:56.257 --> 00:12:00.010
in Gatling guns on
helicopters, and airplanes.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.180
You know, F-22s, F-18s.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.807
These things are highly stable,

00:12:04.807 --> 00:12:06.267
even the high-explosive ones.

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:07.727
So when they hit a tank,

00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:09.895
then they will fragment and explode,

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:11.814
but pulling them out of a leg

00:12:11.814 --> 00:12:13.399
is not going to cause an explosion.

00:12:13.607 --> 00:12:14.775
Clamping or repairing?

00:12:14.775 --> 00:12:15.693
Clamping.

00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:20.948
And that's a round, not a projectile.

00:12:20.948 --> 00:12:22.825
They've got its casing still in the back.

00:12:22.825 --> 00:12:25.703
It's only the projectile
that comes out of the barrel.

00:12:25.703 --> 00:12:27.788
And those blue gloves are not sterile.

00:12:27.788 --> 00:12:29.999
The blue gloves are
just disposable gloves.

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:32.001
They don't have hats on, masks on.

00:12:32.001 --> 00:12:33.961
They're doing this in the
lobby or somewhere like that.

00:12:33.961 --> 00:12:37.131
And I worked in Los Angeles
in that ER for five years,

00:12:37.131 --> 00:12:39.508
and I started the Navy
trauma-training center there,

00:12:39.508 --> 00:12:41.677
and the lobby never
looked that nice to me.

00:12:44.346 --> 00:12:45.347
She's hemorrhaging.

00:12:46.932 --> 00:12:48.893
In the military, in a situation where

00:12:48.893 --> 00:12:51.187
there's an actual explosive in the body,

00:12:51.187 --> 00:12:54.398
we will set them off to the
side outside of the facility,

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:55.649
where no one's going to get hurt,

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:57.443
take care of everybody that
needs to get taken care of

00:12:57.443 --> 00:12:58.569
urgently, emergently,

00:12:58.569 --> 00:13:01.030
get a team of volunteers to go out there.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.741
They'd put a stronghold around
them and then do the thing

00:13:03.741 --> 00:13:05.409
for the metal that they're going to get.

00:13:05.409 --> 00:13:08.579
In a civilian setting, none
of this stuff makes any sense.

00:13:08.579 --> 00:13:11.081
I'd give this a zero out of 10.

00:13:11.957 --> 00:13:13.584
Let me see, let me see.

00:13:15.044 --> 00:13:16.212
Nicked the artery.

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:22.092
It'd be OK if he didn't
say there was an artery,

00:13:22.092 --> 00:13:24.011
but didn't look like it
was bleeding much at all.

00:13:24.011 --> 00:13:26.555
And there was not a swelling
or a hematoma underneath it.

00:13:26.555 --> 00:13:29.350
So from a medical
standpoint of all of this,

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.185
that wound there won't kill him.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:37.107
And then his sewing, the
techniques were not good.

00:13:37.107 --> 00:13:40.402
Civilians have a tendency
to try to use in the movies

00:13:40.402 --> 00:13:42.947
the ringed forceps, the ringed instruments

00:13:42.947 --> 00:13:44.406
in the thumb and then the first finger.

00:13:44.406 --> 00:13:46.242
It's usually for your fourth
finger, and you control

00:13:46.242 --> 00:13:48.244
the rest of the instruments
with the other two.

00:13:52.289 --> 00:13:55.084
Doesn't tie a knot, and he bites it off.

00:13:55.084 --> 00:13:57.336
When you sew, you could do a
running stitch, which is fast,

00:13:57.336 --> 00:13:59.964
but then he bites off and
then leaves that thing down

00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:01.632
without tying at least the knot.

00:14:01.632 --> 00:14:03.509
So that's going to unravel.

00:14:03.509 --> 00:14:05.594
Here, just below my floating rib.

00:14:05.594 --> 00:14:06.804
Be sure not to hit my --

00:14:07.763 --> 00:14:08.931
All right.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:11.725
Be sure not to graze my --

00:14:11.725 --> 00:14:13.477
That's a little too medial for me.

00:14:13.477 --> 00:14:14.770
He hit him right in the clavicle.

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:16.397
Up here, if it goes into clavicle,

00:14:16.397 --> 00:14:17.773
it could be very problematic.

00:14:17.773 --> 00:14:18.858
And then you get a little lower,

00:14:18.858 --> 00:14:19.859
you can get a lot of nerves.

00:14:19.859 --> 00:14:23.237
Choice locations are OK.

00:14:23.237 --> 00:14:24.780
Medical-wise, obviously he did

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.198
such a bad job of doing that up there.

00:14:26.198 --> 00:14:27.908
I'd give it a five, five out of 10.

00:14:27.908 --> 00:14:28.909
None of that's going to do any good

00:14:28.909 --> 00:14:30.828
until we get that arrow out of him.

00:14:30.828 --> 00:14:31.662
Good.

00:14:34.623 --> 00:14:35.875
OK, got it.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.170
So, that doesn't make any sense to me.

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:42.464
The arrow, if he got shot front to back,

00:14:42.464 --> 00:14:45.885
it has already caused the
damage where it resides.

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:48.220
So why would you pull
it out the other side?

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:50.848
The only time I would pull it through

00:14:50.848 --> 00:14:54.768
is if it's a target arrow
and it doesn't have a tip

00:14:54.768 --> 00:14:57.104
and there's feathers in the
back that you can't pull out.

00:14:57.104 --> 00:14:59.106
Now, that's got a razor tip to it.

00:14:59.106 --> 00:15:01.317
If it went front and
didn't come all the way out

00:15:01.317 --> 00:15:02.776
to the back, it didn't pierce the skin,

00:15:02.776 --> 00:15:05.029
it has already injured
what it's going to injure.

00:15:05.029 --> 00:15:06.030
Just pull it out the front.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.324
Why cause more injuries
by cutting him surgically

00:15:08.324 --> 00:15:10.284
and then cutting more muscle?

00:15:10.284 --> 00:15:13.954
You just go get it again for -- ah, wow.

00:15:13.954 --> 00:15:15.748
Distilled spirits is 40%.

00:15:15.748 --> 00:15:17.750
So it has some antibacterial properties.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.710
You won't get any
contaminants into the wound,

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:23.797
whether you use rum, vodka,
gin, whiskey, bourbon.

00:15:23.797 --> 00:15:24.590
It doesn't matter.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:26.717
I give this a four out of 10 max.

00:15:26.717 --> 00:15:27.551
Thanks for watching.

00:15:27.551 --> 00:15:30.054
If you enjoyed this video,
click on the link above.

